.TH DM 1 "2026-11-25" "dm" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dm \- durable terminal multiplexer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dm
[\fIcommand\fP] [\fIoptions\fP]
.br
.B dm
[\fB\-s\fP \fIname\fP]
.br
.B dm
[\fB\-L\fP \fIlayout\fP] [\fB\-s\fP \fIname\fP] [\fB\-\-force\fP]
.SH COMMANDS
A command is the first word after dm. With no command, dm attaches to a session, creating it if needed.
.TP
\fBdm [-s name] [-L layout [--force]]\fP
attach to a session, creating it if needed
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.TP
\fBdm attach [-s name] [-L layout [--force]]\fP
attach to a session, creating it if needed
.br
Aliases: a.
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.TP
\fBdm new [-s name] [-L layout [--force]]\fP
start a session without attaching
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.TP
\fBdm ls\fP
list sessions with pane counts and project
.br
Aliases: list-sessions.
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--json\fP print JSON
.TP
\fBdm web [-s name] [--addr host:port] [--token t] [--read-only]\fP
serve the session to a browser, for a phone on your tailnet
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--addr host:port\fP listen address
.br
\fB--token t\fP access token, generated when empty
.br
\fB--read-only\fP refuse keys, messages and actions
.br
Example: \fBdm web --addr 100.71.227.52:8099\fP
.TP
\fBdm kill [-s name]\fP
stop a session's server (panes survive)
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.TP
\fBdm purge [--dry-run]\fP
remove dead sessions whose panes are gone
.br
\fB--dry-run\fP print what would be removed
.TP
\fBdm reset [-s name | --all] --force\fP
discard a session and its panes for a full restart
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--all\fP reset every session
.br
\fB--force\fP confirm the discard
.TP
\fBdm exec '<json>' [-s name]\fP
send an action as JSON
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--action-json\fP action JSON
.br
Example: \fBdm exec '{"action":"split","target":"w1p1","dir":"right"}'\fP
.TP
\fBdm layout save <name> [-s name] [--force]\fP
capture a session's shape as a named layout file
.br
\fB--s name\fP session the layout is captured from
.br
\fB--force\fP overwrite an existing layout file
.br
Example: \fBdm layout save work -s base\fP
.TP
\fBdm layout ls\fP
list layout files with their workspace, window and pane counts
.TP
\fBdm layout show <name>\fP
print a layout file unchanged
.br
Example: \fBdm layout show work\fP
.TP
\fBdm layout rm <name>\fP
delete a layout file
.TP
\fBdm write-to <pane> --msg T\fP
type text into a pane (--enter to accept)
.br
Aliases: write.
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--msg\fP message text
.br
\fB--enter\fP press enter after the message
.br
Example: \fBdm write-to w1p1 --msg 'ls -la' --enter\fP
.TP
\fBdm split <pane> --dir D\fP
split a pane: left right up down
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--dir D\fP split direction: left right up down
.br
\fB--argv CMD\fP run CMD as the new pane's program; repeat once per word
.br
\fB--json\fP print JSON
.TP
\fBdm focus <pane>\fP
focus a pane
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.TP
\fBdm next-pane\fP
focus the next pane
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.TP
\fBdm prev-pane\fP
focus the previous pane
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.TP
\fBdm plugin list\fP
list the plugins the config declares
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--json\fP print the server's JSON
.TP
\fBdm plugin start <name> [--split | --window] [--target <pane>]\fP
open a plugin pane
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--split\fP open beside the focused pane (the default)
.br
\fB--window\fP open in a new window
.br
\fB--target PANE\fP the pane to open beside, or the source window
.TP
\fBdm rename-workspace <ws> <name>\fP
rename a workspace
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.TP
\fBdm set-project <ws> [name]\fP
set a workspace's project, or clear it with no name
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
Example: \fBdm set-project s1 dm\fP
.TP
\fBdm new-window [--argv CMD]...\fP
open a new window
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--argv CMD\fP run CMD as the new pane's program; repeat once per word
.TP
\fBdm next-window\fP
focus the next window
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.TP
\fBdm prev-window\fP
focus the previous window
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.TP
\fBdm status [pane]\fP
show OSC 7501 agent status
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.TP
\fBdm list\fP
print panes as a table, or the raw tree with --json
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--json\fP print JSON
.TP
\fBdm dash [-s name]\fP
print the palette rows
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.TP
\fBdm peek <id> [--lines N]\fP
print the screen a palette row lands on
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--lines N\fP number of screen lines
.TP
\fBdm wait <pane> [--timeout D]\fP
stream a pane's output until a command ends, the next one when nothing runs, then name the exit code
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--timeout D\fP quiet-idle limit, not total
.br
\fB--json\fP print JSON
.br
Example: \fBdm wait w1p1 --timeout 10m\fP
.TP
\fBdm read <pane> [--lines N]\fP
print a pane's screen
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--lines N\fP number of screen lines
.br
\fB--scrollback N\fP history rows above the screen
.br
\fBdm read <pane> --scrollback N\fP print N rows above the screen
.TP
\fBdm yank <pane> [--lines N]\fP
print a pane's output since the last Enter
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--lines N\fP number of output lines
.br
Example: \fBdm yank w1p1\fP
.TP
\fBdm msg post --state S [--key K] [--msg T] [--body T | --body-file P]\fP
post one entry to the box
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--state S\fP box state: note working blocked done error
.br
\fB--key K\fP box key
.br
\fB--msg\fP message text
.br
\fB--body\fP long body text under the message line
.br
\fB--body-file P\fP read the body from a path, - for stdin
.br
Example: \fBdm msg post --key build --state done --msg 'all green'\fP
.TP
\fBdm msg list [--key K | --prefix P] [--after SEQ] [--limit N] [--json]\fP
print the box newest first
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--key K\fP box key
.br
\fB--prefix P\fP box key prefix
.br
\fB--after SEQ\fP box seq cursor: entries after this one
.br
\fB--limit N\fP list rows, 0 for the whole box
.br
\fB--json\fP print JSON
.TP
\fBdm msg show --key K [--seq N | --from PANE]\fP
print one entry's body, or its summary line
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--key K\fP box key
.br
\fB--seq N\fP the entry's seq
.br
\fB--from PANE\fP the posting pane
.br
Example: \fBdm msg show --key build --seq 12\fP
.TP
\fBdm msg wait [--key K | --prefix P] [--state S] [--after SEQ] [--timeout 5m] [--json]\fP
block until an entry matches
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--key K\fP box key
.br
\fB--prefix P\fP box key prefix
.br
\fB--state S\fP box state: note working blocked done error
.br
\fB--after SEQ\fP box seq cursor: entries after this one
.br
\fB--timeout D\fP wait timeout
.br
\fB--json\fP print JSON
.br
Example: \fBdm msg wait --key build --state done --timeout 10m\fP
.TP
\fBdm msg sub [--key K | --prefix P] [--state S] [--after SEQ] [--json]\fP
stream entries as they arrive
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--key K\fP box key
.br
\fB--prefix P\fP box key prefix
.br
\fB--state S\fP box state: note working blocked done error
.br
\fB--after SEQ\fP box seq cursor: entries after this one
.br
\fB--json\fP print JSON
.br
Example: \fBdm msg sub --prefix build --json\fP
.TP
\fBdm msg clear [--key K | --prefix P]\fP
clear one key, or a prefix branch, or the whole box
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--key K\fP box key
.br
\fB--prefix P\fP box key prefix
.TP
\fBdm msg watchers [--json]\fP
print the live watches
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--json\fP print JSON
.TP
\fBdm msg schema\fP
print the box schema the server serves
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.TP
\fBdm ask list [--json]\fP
print the questions the session lists, oldest first
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--json\fP print JSON
.TP
\fBdm ask open --pane P --tool-call ID [--json]\fP
open an ask on a pane, reading {questions} on stdin
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--pane P\fP pane the ask belongs to
.br
\fB--tool-call ID\fP the agent's tool call the questions belong to
.br
\fB--json\fP print JSON
.br
Example: \fBecho '{"questions":[{"question":"Which base?","header":"base","options":[{"label":"main"}]}]}' | dm ask open --pane w1p1 --tool-call toolu_1\fP
.TP
\fBdm ask await <id> [--timeout 25s] [--json]\fP
block until the ask is answered, or print pending
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--timeout D\fP wait timeout, capped at 25s
.br
\fB--json\fP print JSON
.br
Example: \fBdm ask await a1 --json\fP
.TP
\fBdm ask answer <id> [--json]\fP
answer an ask, reading {items,globalNote,cancel} on stdin
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--json\fP print JSON
.br
Example: \fBecho '{"items":[{"questionIndex":0,"optionIndexes":[0]}]}' | dm ask answer a1\fP
.TP
\fBdm ask close <id> [--json]\fP
drop an ask from the store, idempotently
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--json\fP print JSON
.TP
\fBdm secret register <name> --tool op --ref R [--upstream URL --header T] [--env K=V]...\fP
register a secret: how to fetch it and where it rides
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--tool op\fP tool that prints the value: op or pass
.br
\fB--ref R\fP reference the tool resolves: an op:// URI or a store path
.br
\fB--upstream URL\fP pin the proxy upstream the secret rides to
.br
\fB--header T\fP proxy header template, {} marks the value slot
.br
\fB--env K=V\fP extra environment entry for the tool; repeatable, credential names refused
.br
Example: \fBdm secret register gh-cli --tool op --ref 'op://Personal/github/token' --upstream https://api.github.com --header 'Authorization: Bearer {}'\fP
.TP
\fBdm secret list\fP
print registered secret names and references only
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.TP
\fBdm secret revoke <name>\fP
remove a secret and drop its value everywhere
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.TP
\fBdm secret use <name>\fP
fetch a secret, redact it in every pane, and print the proxy endpoint URL when the secret rides one
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
Example: \fBdm secret use gh-cli\fP
.TP
\fBdm secret hydrate <name> --to P\fP
fetch a secret and write it to a file at 0600
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--to P\fP file path to write
.TP
\fBdm secret type <name> --to PANE\fP
fetch a secret and type it into a pane
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--to PANE\fP target pane id
.br
Example: \fBdm secret type gh-cli --to w1p1\fP
.TP
\fBdm secret sync [--from M] [--trust]\fP
apply a .dm-secrets.kdl mapping: register its secrets, ingest its files, redact everywhere
.br
\fB--s name\fP session name
.br
\fB--from M\fP mapping path, default .dm-secrets.kdl in the working directory
.br
\fB--trust\fP trust a mapping outside the config directory: allow secret blocks and file paths anywhere
.br
Example: \fBdm secret sync\fP
.TP
\fBdm man [-o path]\fP
print the man page, or write it to a file
.br
\fB--o path\fP write the page to this path, or - for stdout
.TP
\fBdm llms [-o path]\fP
print llms.txt, or write it to a file
.br
\fB--o path\fP write the document to this path, or - for stdout
.TP
\fBdm html [-o path]\fP
open this page as HTML in a browser, or write it to a file
.br
\fB--o path\fP write the page to this path, or - for stdout
.br
Example: \fBdm html -o dm.html\fP
.SH ACTIONS
dm exec takes one JSON object. The fields are:
.TP
\fBaction\fP
the action name, required
.TP
\fBtarget\fP
the pane, window or workspace id the action acts on
.TP
\fBdir\fP
split or focus direction: left, right, up or down; swap-panes, move-window and move-workspace swap by direction instead of other
.TP
\fBfg\fP
term-colours: the foreground colour the client terminal reported
.TP
\fBbg\fP
term-colours: the background colour the client terminal reported
.TP
\fBother\fP
the second pane: resize-split, swap-panes and move-pane
.TP
\fBmsg\fP
text to type into a pane
.TP
\fBenter\fP
press enter after msg
.TP
\fBname\fP
a new name for a window, workspace or pane
.TP
\fBwindow\fP
new-workspace: the window name
.TP
\fBindex\fP
select-window: the window number; set-rail: the rail width
.TP
\fBid\fP
request id, echoed in the reply
.TP
\fBcwd\fP
working directory for a new pane, window or workspace
.TP
\fBratio\fP
split ratio, 0 to 1
.TP
\fBnofocus\fP
do not move focus to the new pane
.TP
\fBlayout\fP
select-layout: even-horizontal, even-vertical, main-horizontal, main-vertical or tiled; next cycles the stored preset forward
.TP
\fBon\fP
synchronize-panes: true or false; absent toggles
.TP
\fBfloat\fP
float-open and float-close: the client-chosen float id
.TP
\fBcommand\fP
float-open: the command to run instead of the diff command
.TP
\fBlabel\fP
float-open: the float title, default the command's first word
.TP
\fBargv\fP
split, new-window and new-workspace: the program and arguments a new pane runs instead of a shell
.TP
\fBcols\fP
terminal columns
.TP
\fBrows\fP
terminal rows
.TP
\fBkeys\fP
raw bytes to write to a pane
.TP
\fBdata\fP
raw bytes
.TP
\fBcount\fP
history rows above the screen
.TP
\fBres\fP
the verb payload for the msg-* actions; set-secrets carries the redaction values
.TP
\fBtool\fP
secret-register: the tool that prints the value, op or pass
.TP
\fBref\fP
secret-register: the op:// URI or store path the tool resolves
.TP
\fBupstream\fP
secret-register: the proxy upstream URL the secret rides to
.TP
\fBheader\fP
secret-register: the proxy header template, {} marks the value slot
.TP
\fBpath\fP
secret-hydrate: the file the value is written to, out of band
.TP
\fBtrust\fP
secret-sync: true trusts a mapping outside the config directory, false enforces the untrusted rule; absent resolves it from the mapping's path
.TP
\fBenv\fP
secret-register: extra environment entries for the tool, as KEY=VALUE; credential names are refused
.PP
The action field names one of these. Each example is one JSON object, carrying only the fields that action needs.
