.TP
\fBunknown colour\fP
unknown state colour. Default \fI#8f93a8\fP.
.TP
\fBshell glyph\fP
shell state glyph; also replaces the Nerd Font form. Default \fI▶\fP.
.TP
\fBshell colour\fP
shell state colour. Default \fI#8f93a8\fP.
.SH PROMPT MARKS
dm copies a command's output with prefix y and dm yank. The Enter dm forwards marks where that output starts; the shell's OSC 133 marks make both edges exact. Without them the copy starts after the echoed command line and ends at the last row with content, so the trailing prompt is copied too. With them the copy is the command's output alone, even when the prompt repaints its line on Enter.
.PP
zsh emits the marks with one line in ~/.zshrc, before the prompt theme registers its own precmd hook:
.PP
.nf
precmd() { print -Pn '\ee]133;D\ee\e\e\ee]133;A\ee\e\e' }; preexec() { print -Pn '\ee]133;C\ee\e\e' }
.fi
.PP
dm reads these marks:
.TP
\fBOSC 133;A\fP
the prompt starts. Without a D mark, this is where the output ends.
.TP
\fBOSC 133;C\fP
the command starts, and its output begins here. The copy starts here.
.TP
\fBOSC 133;D\fP
the command ended. The copy ends here.
.PP
The marks are ordinary output, so they travel over ssh and a nested multiplexer like any other bytes. A shell on a remote host needs the same line in its own start file; a pane running a program instead of a shell has no prompt and no marks. dm reads the marks per pane and needs no configuration of its own.
.PP
The marks are defined by the semantic prompts proposal: https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/Per_Bothner/specifications/blob/master/proposals/semantic-prompts.md
.SH LAYOUTS
A layout is a named JSON document that holds a session's shape: its workspaces, their windows, the split trees and each pane's launch data. A file lives at $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dm/layouts/<name>.json, or $HOME/.config/dm/layouts/<name>.json when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset. The name follows the session-name rule: letters, digits, underscore or hyphen. dm layout save <name> captures the shape: from the session's server when one answers, and from its state.json when the session is stopped.
.PP
dm -L <name> starts a session from the file and attaches to it. The session defaults to the layout name, so the same command twice reattaches instead of stacking sessions. A layout seeds an empty session: when the session already has state, that state wins and the file is not applied, so a layout patches nothing. --force resets the session first, then replays the file over the reset.
.PP
dm layout save <name> [-s name] [--force] captures a session, dm -L <name> applies one, and dm layout ls, show and rm list, print and delete the files.
.PP
Only the launch argv is durable. A pane that began as a shell and now runs pi reopens as a shell, because the program a pane runs is recorded at launch, not what it later became. The session's box, its secrets, the agent state and the rail width never travel.
.PP
One layout file:
.nf
{
  "version": 1,
  "name": "go-backend",
  "workspaces": [{
    "name": "development",
    "project": "go-backend",
    "active": 0,
    "windows": [{
      "name": "api",
      "focus": "w1p1",
      "root": {
        "dir": "col",
        "sizes": [0.6, 0.4],
        "kids": [{"pane": "w1p1"}, {"pane": "w1p2"}]
      }
    }]
  }],
  "panes": {
    "w1p1": {"cwd": "/home/b/Code/Personal/go-backend", "argv": ["pi"]},
    "w1p2": {"cwd": "/home/b/Code/Personal/go-backend"}
  }
}
.fi
.SH FILES
Paths use $DM_STATE and $XDG_CONFIG_HOME as ENVIRONMENT describes.
.TP
\fB$DM_STATE/sessions/<name>/state.json\fP
Session state: workspaces, windows, panes, layouts and names.
.TP
\fB$DM_STATE/sessions/<name>/server.sock\fP
Unix socket the session server listens on.
.TP
\fB$DM_STATE/sessions/<name>/panes/<id>.sock\fP
Unix socket one pane's mux listens on.
.TP
\fB$DM_STATE/sessions/<name>/panes/<id>.log\fP
Pane output log.
.TP
\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dm/config.kdl\fP
Holds prefix, keymaps, commands, plugins, default-fold, tab-min-width, tab-max-width, padding, history-lines, glyphs, window-label, dashboard-preview, palette-rows, ask-rows, ask-cols, ask-split, diff-command, web-addr, dashboard-layout, dashboard-peek, shelf-layout, shelf-size, float-shadow and theme name; written on first run.
.TP
\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dm/themes/<name>.kdl\fP
Theme palette, accents, glyphs and state styles.
.TP
\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dm/layouts/<name>.json\fP
A captured layout: workspaces, windows, split trees and each pane's launch cwd and argv. Written by dm layout save.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBDM_STATE\fP
State directory; overrides XDG_STATE_HOME.
.TP
\fBXDG_STATE_HOME\fP
State directory base; dm uses $XDG_STATE_HOME/dm.
.TP
\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fP
Config directory base; dm reads $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dm/config.kdl.
.TP
\fBHOME\fP
Home directory base; dm reads $HOME/.config/dm and $HOME/.local/state/dm when the XDG variables are unset.
.TP
\fBDM_SESSION\fP
The session a pane belongs to; dm refuses to attach that session from inside it, and refuses a name with no live session, which would nest a new one in the pane.
.TP
\fBDM_PANE\fP
The pane id a pane's shell runs in.
.TP
\fBDM_NERD\fP
1 forces Nerd Font glyphs, 0 forces the Unicode fallback.
.TP
\fBDM_NOTIFY\fP
0 turns desktop notifications off.
.TP
\fBDM_STATS\fP
Non-empty prints two stderr lines when the client exits: "dm: style cache: <hits> hits, <misses> misses, <rate>% hit rate" and "dm: pacing: <frames> frames written, <wakes> wakes, <ms>ms interval".
.TP
\fBCOLUMNS\fP
Terminal width when the client cannot ask the terminal.
.TP
\fBSHELL\fP
The shell a new pane runs.
.TP
\fBTMUX\fP
Set inside tmux; dm uses it to pick its terminal behaviour.

A shell dm starts, a pane's or a float's, inherits a short allowlist of the server's environment: PATH, HOME, USER, LOGNAME, SHELL, TERM, TERM_PROGRAM, COLORTERM, LANG, LC_*, TZ, TMPDIR, XDG_*, WAYLAND_DISPLAY, DISPLAY, SSH_AUTH_SOCK, XAUTHORITY and DM_STATE, plus a *_SOCK name with an absolute path value when the name is not credential-shaped. Everything else is dropped, and dm sets DM_PANE and DM_SESSION itself. Each entry is a name a shell, a terminal or an X client needs before the shell reads its rc file, or the dm state root that a dm command in the shell needs; the rc file can re-export what else a session needs.
.SH SEE ALSO
